changes.adapted_text:
The velocity of an incompressible fluid can remain bounded even as its vorticity, the curl of the velocity, becomes unbounded. We construct such a flow from smooth data on $\mathbb T^3$, the cube $[-1/2,1/2]^3$ with opposite faces identified. Its velocity $u$ and pressure $p$ solve the unforced Euler equations $$\begin{equation}
 \partial_tu+(u\cdot\nabla)u+\nabla p=0,
 \qquad \operatorname{div}u=0
 \quad\hbox{on }\mathbb T^3\times[0,T_*). \tag{1.1}
\end{equation}$$ For smooth divergence-free initial data the local theory gives a unique smooth solution on a maximal time interval $[0,T_*)$ \[[13](#cite-EbinMarsden), §15.2(i),(iii), pp. 150–151\]. Our data are odd. The particle at the origin therefore stays at rest, even as the vorticity there grows.

**Theorem 1.1.** There is an odd divergence-free $u_0\in C^\infty(\mathbb T^3;\mathbb R^3)$ whose maximal classical solution $u$ of (1.1) is smooth and odd on $\mathbb T^3\times[0,T_*)$ for a finite $T_*>0$, and satisfies $$\begin{equation}
 \int_0^{T_*}\|\nabla u(t)\|_{L^\infty}\,\mathrm dt=\infty,
 \qquad
 \int_0^{T_*}\|\operatorname{curl}u(t)\|_{L^\infty}\,\mathrm dt=\infty. \tag{1.2}
\end{equation}$$ Moreover, $$\begin{equation}
 \sup_{0\le t<T_*}\|u(t)\|_{L^\infty}<\infty,
 \qquad \lim_{t\uparrow T_*}|\operatorname{curl}u(t,0)|=\infty. \tag{1.3}
\end{equation}$$ For every compact $K\subset\mathbb T^3\setminus\{0\}$ and every integer $m\ge0$, both $u(t)$ and $\partial_tu(t)$ converge in $C^m(K)$ as $t\uparrow T_*$.

changes.authors_text:
The goal of this work is to construct smooth initial data for the incompressible Euler equations on the three-dimensional torus whose solution blows up in finite time. We represent $\mathbb T^3$ by the cube $[-1/2,1/2]^3$ with periodic boundary conditions (opposite faces identified). We write these equations as $$\begin{equation}
 \partial_tu+(u\cdot\nabla)u+\nabla p=0,
 \qquad \operatorname{div}u=0
 \quad\hbox{on }\mathbb T^3\times[0,T_*). \tag{1.1}
\end{equation}$$ Local existence and uniqueness for sufficiently regular data go back to Lichtenstein and Günther in the 1920s \[[43](#cite-Lichtenstein1925), [44](#cite-Gunther1926)\]. Classical theory on $\mathbb T^3$ supplies a unique maximal smooth solution from smooth divergence-free data \[[13](#cite-EbinMarsden), [10](#cite-BedrossianVicol)\]. By the Beale–Kato–Majda criterion \[[9](#cite-BKM), [11](#cite-GGPP)\], a smooth solution on $[0,T)$ continues smoothly through $T<\infty$ if $\int_0^T\|\operatorname{curl}u(t)\|_{L^\infty}\,\,\mathrm dt<\infty$.

On $\mathbb R^3$, Elgindi \[[15](#cite-ElgindiSingularity)\] constructed finite-time blow-up from $C^{1,\alpha_0}$ initial data for some $\alpha_0>0$. Elgindi, Ghoul and Masmoudi \[[16](#cite-ElgindiGhoulMasmoudi)\] obtained stable, locally self-similar blow-up for finite-energy data in this regularity class. Chen and Hou \[[28](#cite-ChenHouSmoothI), [29](#cite-ChenHouSmoothII)\] prove nearly self-similar blow-up from smooth axisymmetric data in a periodic cylinder with boundary, using computer-assisted estimates. The theorem below concerns smooth periodic data on $\mathbb T^3$, with bounded velocity and vorticity growth concentrated at the origin.

**Theorem 1.1.** There is an odd divergence-free $u_0\in C^\infty(\mathbb T^3;\mathbb R^3)$ whose maximal classical solution $u$ of (1.1) is smooth and odd on $\mathbb T^3\times[0,T_*)$ for a finite $T_*>0$, and satisfies $$\begin{equation}
 \int_0^{T_*}\|\nabla u(t)\|_{L^\infty}\,\mathrm dt=\infty,
 \qquad
 \int_0^{T_*}\|\operatorname{curl}u(t)\|_{L^\infty}\,\mathrm dt=\infty. \tag{1.2}
\end{equation}$$ Moreover, $$\begin{equation}
 \sup_{0\le t<T_*}\|u(t)\|_{L^\infty}<\infty,
 \qquad \lim_{t\uparrow T_*}|\operatorname{curl}u(t,0)|=\infty. \tag{1.3}
\end{equation}$$ For every compact $K\subset\mathbb T^3\setminus\{0\}$ and every integer $m\ge0$, both $u(t)$ and $\partial_tu(t)$ converge in $C^m(K)$ as $t\uparrow T_*$.

**Remark 1.2.** The solution in Theorem 1.1 may be chosen so that it extends smoothly in space and time through $T_*$ away from the origin. A proof is given in the auxiliary note \[[31](#cite-TerminalSmoothness)\].

We construct a sequence of smooth approximate solutions of the Euler equations whose residuals tend to zero. The sequence converges in every spatial $C^m$ norm, uniformly on compact time intervals before $T_*$, to a solution of the Euler equations.

At each step in the sequence we add an oscillation at a higher frequency, localized near the origin and initially small in velocity. The velocity gradient of the preceding scales amplifies the new oscillation, whose gradient in turn drives the growth of the next one. The growth intervals have finite total length. This multiscale construction follows the approach of Córdoba and Martínez-Zoroa for the Euler equations with forcing and for forced IPM \[[18](#cite-CMZForcedEuler), [19](#cite-CMZForcedIPM)\]. Córdoba, Martínez-Zoroa and Zheng \[[17](#cite-CMZUnforcedEuler)\] used a cascade of vorticity layers to obtain blow-up for the unforced Euler equations on $\mathbb R^3$ from $C^{1,\alpha}$ data smooth away from the origin. Related constructions give blow-up with forcing for the inviscid Boussinesq equations, the variable-density Euler equations and the hypodissipative Navier–Stokes equations \[[20](#cite-CLMZForcedBoussinesq), [21](#cite-CLMZNonhomogeneousEuler), [22](#cite-CMZHypodissipativeNS)\]. Our companion papers proving singularity formation for forced IPM, the forced Boussinesq equations, and the forced Euler equations \[[3](#cite-AlpogeBuckmasterCoiculescuIPM), [1](#cite-AlpogeBuckmasterBoussinesq), [2](#cite-AlpogeBuckmasterForcedEuler)\] use forces smooth in space and time up to the singular time. In the present construction we use Craik–Criminale waves as building blocks and choose the initial velocity at each step so that the limiting solution is unforced.

changes.adapted_text:
Consider first a flow on $\mathbb R^3$ of the form $U(t,x)=D(t)x$. It solves the Euler equations if the smooth matrix $D$ has trace zero and $D'+D^2$ is symmetric. Indeed, the acceleration is $(D'+D^2)x$, the negative gradient of $p_D=-x\cdot(D'+D^2)x/2$. Craik and Criminale \[[12](#cite-CraikCriminale), §2, (2.4)–(2.10)\] superposed an exact plane wave on such flows. In the inviscid case its velocity takes the form $$w(t,x)=\mathbf a(t)F(k(t)\cdot x),\qquad \mathbf a\cdot k=0.$$ Here $F$ is smooth, odd and $1$-periodic, with $F'(0)=1$, for example $F(s)=\sin(2\pi s)/(2\pi)$. At any one time the planes $k\cdot x=s$ carry a common velocity $\mathbf a F(s)$, tangent to each plane. A displacement of length $1/|k|$ in the direction $k/|k|$ advances the phase by one period. Thus the speed is at most $|\mathbf a|\|F\|_\infty$, whereas the velocity gradient at the origin is $\mathbf a\otimes k$.

The wave advects no part of its own velocity, since motion along $\mathbf a$ leaves the phase unchanged. Transporting the phase by $Dx$ and balancing the remaining acceleration against the pressure gives $$\begin{equation}
 k'=-D^Tk,\qquad
 \mathbf a'=-D\mathbf a+p_{\rm w} k,\qquad
 p_{\rm w}=\frac{2k\cdot D\mathbf a}{|k|^2}. \tag{1.4}
\end{equation}$$ The last formula follows by differentiating $k\cdot\mathbf a=0$. With $P'=F$, the added pressure is $-p_{\rm w}P(k\cdot x)$, and $Dx+w$ solves the Euler equations exactly. Related short-wave instabilities of planar vortices were studied by Pierrehumbert \[[35](#cite-Pierrehumbert1986)\] and Bayly \[[36](#cite-Bayly1986)\].

changes.authors_text:
We first describe the wave construction of Craik and Criminale \[[12](#cite-CraikCriminale)\]. Let $D(t)$ be a smooth matrix with $\operatorname{tr}D=0$ and $D'+D^2$ symmetric. These conditions ensure that $U(t,x)=D(t)x$, linear in space, solves the Euler equations on $\mathbb R^3$, with pressure $p_D(t,x)=-x\cdot(D'+D^2)x/2$. We add to this linear background a velocity $$w(t,x)=\mathbf a(t)F(k(t)\cdot x),\qquad \mathbf a\cdot k=0,$$ where $k(t)\ne0$ is the wave vector, $\mathbf a(t)$ is the amplitude vector, and $F$ is a smooth odd $1$-periodic profile of the scalar phase $s$. For example, one may take $F(s)=\sin(2\pi s)$. The vector $\mathbf a$ records both size and direction: the velocity amplitude is $|\mathbf a|\|F\|_\infty$, and $\mathbf a/|\mathbf a|$ is its unit direction when $\mathbf a\ne0$. The unit wave vector is $k/|k|$. Since $F$ has period one, $w$ is periodic in the direction of $k$ with period $1/|k|$, so $|k|$ is the spatial frequency of the wave.

The sum $u=Dx+w$ is an exact solution of the Euler equations if $k$ and $\mathbf a$ solve $$\begin{equation}
 k'=-D^Tk,\qquad
 \mathbf a'=-D\mathbf a+p_{\rm w} k,\qquad
 p_{\rm w}=\frac{2k\cdot D\mathbf a}{|k|^2}. \tag{1.4}
\end{equation}$$ Its pressure is $p=p_D-p_{\rm w} P(k\cdot x)$, where $P'=F$. Indeed, $\mathbf a\cdot k=0$ gives $(w\cdot\nabla)w=0$, the first ordinary differential equation transports the phase by $Dx$, and the second cancels the remaining acceleration with the gradient of the wave pressure $-p_{\rm w}P(k\cdot x)$. The displayed value of $p_{\rm w}$ preserves $\mathbf a\cdot k=0$. We call (1.4) the Craik–Criminale equations for the wave vector and the amplitude vector. Normalizing $F'(0)=1$ gives $\nabla w(t,0)=\mathbf a\otimes k$. Related short-wave instabilities of planar vortices were studied by Pierrehumbert \[[35](#cite-Pierrehumbert1986)\] and Bayly \[[36](#cite-Bayly1986)\].

