Let $u,p$ be a smooth solution of the Euler equations on $\mathbb T^3\times[0,T]$, with $T>0$. The flow $X(t,a)$ of $u$ gives the position at time $t$ of the particle that starts at $a$. It satisfies $$\partial_tX(t,a)=u(t,X(t,a)),\qquad X(0,a)=a.$$ For a smooth divergence-free velocity correction $b$, let $X_\delta$ be the flow of $u+\delta b$ with the same initial positions, so that $$\partial_tX_\delta(t,a)
       =(u+\delta b)(t,X_\delta(t,a)),\qquad X_\delta(0,a)=a.$$ The first-order change $\xi$ in the particle trajectories is defined by $$X_\delta(t,a)=X(t,a)+\delta\xi(t,a)+O(\delta^2).$$ Differentiating the flow equation at $\delta=0$ gives $$\begin{equation}
 \partial_t\xi(t,a)=\nabla u(t,X(t,a))\xi(t,a)+b(t,X(t,a)),
 \qquad \xi(0,a)=0. \tag{1.5}
\end{equation}$$ The displacement vanishes at time zero because all the flows start from the same particle labels. Their initial velocities can differ, with $\xi_t(0,a)=b(0,a)$. At time $T$, the end of this interval, we require $\xi(T,a)=0$ for every $a$. Thus $X_\delta(T,a)=X(T,a)+O(\delta^2)$, even though the two particles can follow different paths in between.

changes.authors_text:
Let $u,p$ solve the Euler equations on $[0,T]$. The flow $X(t,a)$ of $u$ gives the position at time $t$ of the particle that starts at $a$. It satisfies $$\partial_tX(t,a)=u(t,X(t,a)),\qquad X(0,a)=a.$$ For a divergence-free velocity correction $b$, let $X_\delta$ be the flow of $u+\delta b$ with the same initial positions, so that $$\partial_tX_\delta(t,a)
       =(u+\delta b)(t,X_\delta(t,a)),\qquad X_\delta(0,a)=a.$$ The first-order change $\xi$ in the particle trajectories is defined by $$X_\delta(t,a)=X(t,a)+\delta\xi(t,a)+O(\delta^2).$$ Differentiating the flow equation at $\delta=0$ gives $$\begin{equation}
 \partial_t\xi(t,a)=\nabla u(t,X(t,a))\xi(t,a)+b(t,X(t,a)),
 \qquad \xi(0,a)=0. \tag{1.5}
\end{equation}$$ The initial condition follows from the common starting positions. Since also $X(0,a)=a$, evaluating (1.5) at $t=0$ gives $\partial_t\xi(0,a)=b(0,a)$. This identity holds for every $b$ and imposes no condition. We impose instead the endpoint condition $\xi(T,a)=0$. It states that $X_\delta(T,a)=X(T,a)+O(\delta^2)$: the corrected flow carries each particle to its original final position, up to an error of order $\delta^2$. When $b$ solves the linearized Euler equations (1.6) below, it is determined by $b(0)$ and the source. Under the bounds $H\le\operatorname{Id}$ and $T\le1$ below, the endpoint condition selects $b(0)$ uniquely.

changes.adapted_text:
Given a smooth force $f$, we seek a velocity correction $b$ and a pressure variation $q$ satisfying $$\begin{equation}
 \partial_t b+(u\cdot\nabla)b+(b\cdot\nabla)u+\nabla q=f,
 \qquad \operatorname{div}b=0. \tag{1.6}
\end{equation}$$ The force will cancel the error in the parent and the localized wave. The unknown initial velocity $b(0)$ must give the two displacement conditions above. To see what determines it, differentiate (1.5) along a particle and substitute (1.6). For the exact Euler parent $u$ this gives $$\begin{equation}
 \begin{gathered}
 \xi_{tt}+H\xi+(\nabla q)\circ X=f\circ X,
 \qquad H=(D_x^2p)\circ X,\\
 \operatorname{div}_x(\xi\circ X^{-1})=0,\qquad \xi(0)=\xi(T)=0.
 \end{gathered} \tag{1.7}
\end{equation}$$ At each label $a$, $H$ is the pressure Hessian at the particle’s current position. The bound $H\le\operatorname{Id}$ means $z\cdot H(t,a)z\le|z|^2$ for every vector $z$ and every $(t,a)$. The divergence constraint expresses volume preservation to first order. It applies to $\xi\circ X^{-1}$ because this is the displacement written as a function of the particle’s current position. With $f=0$ the equation is the Jacobi equation for the geodesic in Arnold’s description of Euler flow \[[5](#cite-Arnold1966), Section 10\].

changes.authors_text:
For a prescribed momentum source $f$, the inhomogeneous problem is to find $b,q$ satisfying the linearized Euler equations $$\begin{equation}
 \partial_t b+(u\cdot\nabla)b+(b\cdot\nabla)u+\nabla q=f,
 \qquad \operatorname{div}b=0. \tag{1.6}
\end{equation}$$ Here $q$ is the pressure variation, and the displacement induced by $b$ must satisfy $\xi(0)=\xi(T)=0$. Differentiating (1.5) in time and using (1.6) gives $$\begin{equation}
 \begin{gathered}
 \xi_{tt}+H\xi+(\nabla q)\circ X=f\circ X,
 \qquad H=(D_x^2p)\circ X,\\
 \operatorname{div}_x(\xi\circ X^{-1})=0,\qquad \xi(0)=\xi(T)=0.
 \end{gathered} \tag{1.7}
\end{equation}$$ The homogeneous equation is the Jacobi equation along the geodesic associated with this solution in Arnold’s formulation \[[5](#cite-Arnold1966)\]. The constraint $\operatorname{div}_x(\xi\circ X^{-1})=0$ expresses volume preservation to first order.

changes.adapted_text:
For a solution of (1.7), testing with $-\xi$ and integrating by parts gives the identity $$\begin{equation}
 \underbrace{\int_0^T\!\int_{\mathbb T^3}
   \bigl(|\xi_t|^2-\xi\cdot H\xi\bigr)\,\,\mathrm da\,\,\mathrm dt}_{\mathcal Q(\xi)}
 =-\int_0^T\!\int_{\mathbb T^3}(f\circ X)\cdot\xi\,\,\mathrm da\,\,\mathrm dt. \tag{1.8}
\end{equation}$$ Indeed, changing variables $x=X(t,a)$ changes the pressure pairing to $\int_{\mathbb T^3}\nabla q\cdot(\xi\circ X^{-1})\,\,\mathrm dx=0$. On volume-preserving paths with both endpoint maps fixed, $\mathcal Q$ is the second variation of the kinetic action. The family $X_\delta$ above identifies its tangent displacement $\xi$ and fixes the terminal map to first order.

If $H\le\operatorname{Id}$ and $T\le1$, then $|\xi(t,a)|^2\le(T-t)\int_t^T|\xi_\tau(\tau,a)|^2\,\,\mathrm d\tau$ gives $$\begin{equation}
 \mathcal Q(\xi)\ge\Bigl(1-\frac{T^2}{2}\Bigr)\|\xi_t\|_{L^2_{t,a}}^2
 \ge\frac12\|\xi_t\|_{L^2_{t,a}}^2 . \tag{1.9}
\end{equation}$$ Only a positive value of $\xi\cdot H\xi$ can reduce the energy. A large negative eigenvalue of $H$ increases the contribution from displacements in its eigendirection. Thus the upper bound on $H$ suffices for (1.9). With $f=0$ the energy identity gives $\mathcal Q(\xi)=0$, so the estimate forces $\xi_t=0$. Starting from $\xi(0)=0$ we get $\xi=0$, and then $b=0$ by (1.5). This proves injectivity of the homogeneous endpoint map $b(0)\mapsto\xi(T)$ and hence uniqueness whenever the forced problem has a solution. Brenier used a Dirichlet Poincaré inequality for paths with matching endpoints and an upper pressure-Hessian bound to obtain a criterion for action minimization \[[32](#cite-Brenier1989), Theorem 5.1 and Lemma 5.2, pp. 242–244\]. See also \[[33](#cite-Brenier1999), Theorem 2.4, author version\].

changes.authors_text:
For a solution of (1.7), testing with $-\xi$ and integrating by parts gives the identity $$\begin{equation}
 \underbrace{\int_0^T\!\int_{\mathbb T^3}
   \bigl(|\xi_t|^2-\xi\cdot H\xi\bigr)\,\,\mathrm da\,\,\mathrm dt}_{\mathcal Q(\xi)}
 =-\int_0^T\!\int_{\mathbb T^3}(f\circ X)\cdot\xi\,\,\mathrm da\,\,\mathrm dt. \tag{1.8}
\end{equation}$$ Volume preservation and the divergence constraint remove the pressure variation. The quadratic form $\mathcal Q$ is the fixed-endpoint second variation of the action. If $H\le\operatorname{Id}$ and $T\le1$, then ${|\xi(t,a)|^2\le(T-t)\int_t^T|\xi_\tau(\tau,a)|^2\,\,\mathrm d\tau}$ gives $$\begin{equation}
 \mathcal Q(\xi)\ge\Bigl(1-\frac{T^2}{2}\Bigr)\|\xi_t\|_{L^2_{t,a}}^2
 \ge\frac12\|\xi_t\|_{L^2_{t,a}}^2 . \tag{1.9}
\end{equation}$$ The term $-\xi\cdot H\xi$ lowers the integrand where $\xi\cdot H\xi>0$ and raises it where $\xi\cdot H\xi<0$. Thus an upper bound for $H$ gives the lower bound in (1.9). For $f=0$, (1.8) gives $\mathcal Q(\xi)=0$. Then (1.9) forces $\xi_t=0$, and $\xi(0)=0$ gives $\xi=0$. Equation (1.5) gives $b=0$, proving that the linearized endpoint map $b(0)\mapsto\xi(T)$ has trivial kernel. Upper bounds on the pressure Hessian also enter Brenier’s fixed-endpoint action-minimization criterion \[[32](#cite-Brenier1989), [33](#cite-Brenier1999)\].

changes.adapted_text:
A finite step gives an approximate velocity $U$ with pressure $p_U$. Its momentum equation has right-hand side $R_U=\partial_tU+(U\cdot\nabla)U+\nabla p_U$. Differentiating it along the parent flow gives the displacement coefficient $H^\#=(D_x^2p_U-\nabla R_U)\circ X$ (Section 4). Although $\nabla R_U$ can be nonsymmetric, only the symmetric part of $H^\#$ enters $\xi\cdot H^\#\xi$. With $H^\#\le\operatorname{Id}$ in this sense and $0<T\le1$, Theorem 4.2 constructs the correction for each prescribed force on $[0,T]$. The estimate for the inverse is polynomial in the deformation and coefficient norms of this parent.

In the affine wave of Subsection 1.1, the Hessian of $-p_{\rm w}P(k\cdot x)$ is $-p_{\rm w}F'(k\cdot x)k\otimes k$. We seek an odd periodic profile $F_h$ satisfying $$\begin{equation}
 F_h'(0)=1,\qquad -Ch\le F_h'(s)\le1 . \tag{1.10}
\end{equation}$$ The unit slope retains the full shear $\mathbf a\otimes k$ at the origin. In the normal direction $k/|k|$ the wave pressure has curvature $-p_{\rm w}|k|^2F_h'(k\cdot x)$, and it has zero curvature in the tangent directions. When $p_{\rm w}>0$ its large negative curvature at phase zero helps the displacement estimate. Positive curvature can arise only where $F_h'<0$, and is bounded by $$\begin{equation}
 p_{\rm w}|k|^2\bigl(-F_h'(k\cdot x)\bigr)_+
 \le Ch\,p_{\rm w}|k|^2, \tag{1.11}
\end{equation}$$ where $(r)_+=\max\{r,0\}$. Over a period the derivative of $F_h$ must have mean zero. The normalized sine profile has slope $1$ at zero and $-1$ at phase $1/2$. We instead concentrate the positive slope in a narrow interval of width $O(h)$. A shallow negative slope over the rest of the period balances its integral (Figure 2). This also gives $\|F_h\|_\infty=O(h)$, so the wave velocity is $O(h|\mathbf a|)$.

For the localized wave the pressure coefficient is positive along the central trajectory throughout the amplification interval. Estimate (5.19) extends the sign to neighboring labels in the small support of the wave. The spatial cutoff is nonnegative. Thus the coefficient of $-F_h'$ in the leading normal curvature is nonnegative and is bounded by a parent-dependent multiple of $\lambda^{1/4}$. Taking $h=\lambda^{-1/2}$ bounds the positive part by a parent-dependent multiple of $\lambda^{-1/4}$ (Lemma 5.9). Section 7 sums the pressure increments and controls $\|\nabla R_U\|_\infty$ to keep $H^\#\le\operatorname{Id}$ at successive steps.

**Figure 2.** The sine slope divided by its value at the origin (dashed) and the modified slope $F_h'$ (solid). Both equal one at zero. The illustrated plateau $-h/(4-h)$ corresponds to a bump of integral $1/4$ in Lemma 3.1.

changes.authors_text:
For an approximate parent $U$ with residual $R_U$, Subsection 5.1 uses $H^\#=(D_x^2p_U-\nabla R_U)\circ X$ in place of $H$, under the same bounds $H^\#\le\operatorname{Id}$ and $T\le1$. Subsection 4.3 uses the resulting coercivity to construct a bounded inverse for the forced Jacobi problem, and Appendix A establishes the quantitative Hölder bounds of Theorem 4.2. The inverse exists on every interval $[0,T]$ with $0<T\le1$ on which $H^\#\le\operatorname{Id}$, with a bound that depends on the parent but requires no further smallness of $T$. Each correction therefore solves a linear two-point boundary value problem along the flow of the parent, and no short-time argument is used.

In the affine wave of Subsection 1.1, the Hessian of $-p_{\rm w}P(k\cdot x)$ is $-p_{\rm w}F'(k\cdot x)k\otimes k$. We seek an odd periodic profile $F_h$ satisfying $$\begin{equation}
 F_h'(0)=1,\qquad -Ch\le F_h'(s)\le1 . \tag{1.10}
\end{equation}$$ The first condition preserves the wave gradient $\mathbf a\otimes k$ at the origin, and hence the amplification described in Subsection 1.1. The lower bound on $F_h'$ controls the largest eigenvalue of $D_x^2[-p_{\rm w}P_h(k\cdot x)]$, where $P_h'=F_h$. On an interval with $p_{\rm w}>0$, its positive part satisfies $$\begin{equation}
 p_{\rm w}|k|^2\bigl(-F_h'(k\cdot x)\bigr)_+
 \le Ch\,p_{\rm w}|k|^2, \tag{1.11}
\end{equation}$$ where $(r)_+=\max\{r,0\}$. For fixed wave coefficients, taking $h$ small makes this positive part small at every point. In the construction $h$ is a negative power of the frequency of the wave. As $h$ shrinks, the wave produces thin layers of strong shear while its velocity amplitude is $O(h|\mathbf a|)$. Its gradient at the origin remains $\mathbf a\otimes k$, preserving the local shear that amplifies the next wave. The pressure Hessian added at each step is bounded above by a small multiple of the identity. Section 7 sums these bounds and controls $\|\nabla R_U\|_\infty$ to obtain $H^\#\le\operatorname{Id}$ as a quadratic form.

**Figure 2.** The sine slope divided by its value at the origin (dashed) and the slope $F_h'$ of the modified profile (solid). Both curves equal one at the origin.

changes.adapted_text:
With $B_2$ determined, the oscillatory correction $V_2$ and its pressure $\pi_2$ solve $$\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 D_tV_2+(\nabla U)V_2+\kappa\partial_s\pi_2
   &=-(H_2-\langle H_2\rangle_s)-(B_2\cdot\kappa)\partial_s(\widetilde\chi A_1),\\
 \kappa\cdot V_2&=0,\qquad \langle V_2\rangle_s=0.
 \end{split} \tag{1.12}
\end{equation}$$ Throughout the step the phase follows $U$. The additional transport of the leading wave by $B_2$ therefore appears on the right as $-(B_2\cdot\kappa)\partial_s(\widetilde\chi A_1)$. This term has zero phase average because it differentiates a periodic function in $s$. At fixed phase along each trajectory, (1.12) is a forced Craik–Criminale amplitude equation. Every wave uses zero initial data for this correction. For label $a$ and phase $s$ its solution gives the velocity correction $V_2(t,X(t,a),s)$. Perpendicularity to $\kappa$ cancels the fast part of the divergence, leaving the spatial derivatives at fixed phase. The curl construction of Subsection 3.1 gives the divergence correction $C_3$ of $V_2$, so that $V_2+C_3$ is exactly divergence free. The term $C_3$ belongs to level three, where we solve for the mean and oscillatory corrections again.

In the whole-space construction of OpenAI \[[56](#cite-OpenAIEuler2026), §§3.3–3.4, (3.19), (3.27)–(3.28)\], a condition on the initial mean velocity makes it compactly supported. For waves activated at $t_0>0$, the forced transverse correction there has zero displacement at both ends of $[0,t_0]$. Here transverse corrections start with zero velocity at every step.

At step $j$ the expansion stops at a finite depth $P$ chosen for that step in Section 7. We retain $C_{P+1}$ to complete the last oscillation. Each level gains a factor $\lambda^{-2/3}$ up to powers of the parent bound. Once $\lambda$ exceeds a fixed power of that bound, the remainder has the factor $\lambda^{-2P/3}$ (Theorem 5.6). Two other errors enter the momentum equation. Differentiating the time cutoff gives an error proportional to the wave during activation, when its amplitude is at most a fixed multiple of $\exp(-\varepsilon^{-1/2})$ times its peak. Finally we apply a Fourier cutoff to the corrected velocity. We choose the depth, gain and cutoff so that the resulting residual is small on the next wave’s frequency scale (Proposition 5.11). The oscillation and its divergence corrections remain localized before truncation. We must control the means and the truncation error across the whole torus.

**Figure 3.** Building the next parent. Within each level, the mean enters the equation for the oscillation. The divergence correction completes that oscillation and is counted at the next level.

