Define the scaled components $\zeta=(\zeta_1,\zeta_2,\zeta_3)$ and $\alpha=(\alpha_1,\alpha_2,\alpha_3)$ of the wave vector and an amplitude vector by $$\begin{equation}
 k=\lambda\left(\zeta_1E_1+\frac{\zeta_2}{S}E_2+\zeta_3E_3\right),
 \qquad
 \mathbf a=\frac{\alpha_1}{S}E_1+\alpha_2E_2+\frac{\alpha_3}{S}E_3. \tag{2.3}
\end{equation}$$ Then $\zeta(0)=(0,0,1)$ and $$|k|^2=\lambda^2m,\qquad
 m=\zeta_1^2+\zeta_2^2/S^2+\zeta_3^2.$$ Transversality is $\zeta\cdot\alpha=0$, so where $\zeta_3\ne0$, $$\alpha_3=-\frac{\zeta_1\alpha_1+\zeta_2\alpha_2}{\zeta_3}.$$ The two components $\alpha_1,\alpha_2$ therefore determine the amplitude vector. Two small components can contribute at leading order. In the wave pressure, for instance, $\sigma(k\cdot E_2)(\mathbf a\cdot E_1)=2\nu\lambda\zeta_2\alpha_1$, since $\sigma=2\nu S^2$. The factor $S^{-1}$ in the $E_2$ coordinate of $k$ makes the contribution of $B$ to the coefficient of $\zeta_3$ in the equation for $\zeta_2$ equal to $-B_{32}/\nu$, which equals $-\varepsilon$ at $\tau=0$. In the growing mode of Subsection 1.1, the components of the amplitude along $E_1$ and $E_2$ have opposite signs, and the first is $S^{-1}(1-\nu/\sigma)^{-1/2}$ times the second in absolute value. With the scaling in (2.3), this mode is $\alpha_1=-\alpha_2$ to leading order. In Subsection 2.2.2 the components $(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)$ are divided further by the amplitude in the strong-shear limit, which gives the normalized coordinates (2.15).

