On the envelope, (5.19) bounds the seed by twice its size at the origin. The velocity $g\mathbf aF_h$ is smaller than the seed by the factor $\|F_h\|_\infty\le Ch$, so its size is at most $C\lambda^{-5/4}$. At a point with wave vector $\kappa$, a unit change of phase has local normal length $|\kappa|^{-1}$. The steep part of the profile has width $h$ in phase, so differentiating it in space can cost $|\kappa|/h$. The first mean equation removes both the quadratic wave forcing and $R_U$, so we require the parent residual to be at most the square of this scale, $$\begin{equation}
 \left\lVert R_U\right\rVert_{C_tL^\infty}\le\lambda^{-5/2}. \tag{5.6}
\end{equation}$$ The radius of the envelope is a negative power of $\mathsf D$ with an absolute exponent, $\ell_*\le\mathsf D^{-3}$. The exponent is fixed sufficiently large for the finitely many estimates of Subsections 5.2–5.4. In particular the support of $g$ lies in the interior of $[-1/2,1/2]^3$.

