The phase-independent corrections of the expansion solve the linearized Euler equations over the parent, with zero displacement at both ends of $[0,T]$. For a smooth phase-independent source $\bar f(t,a)$, periodic in $a$, Theorem 4.2 over $U$ gives $(B,\bar q)$ with $$\begin{equation}
 B_t+MB+\mathbf d\bar q=\bar f,\qquad \mathbf d\cdot B=0,\qquad
 z(0)=z(T)=0,\quad B=D_aX z_t. \tag{5.11}
\end{equation}$$ Here $\bar f=f\circ X$, $v=z_t$ and $\bar q$ is the pressure in (4.11). The datum for the integrated equation (4.9) is $L(t,a)=-\int_0^t D_aX(s,a)^T\bar f(s,a)\,\,\mathrm ds$. It selects $B(0)$ so that $\int_0^T(D_aX)^{-1}B\,\,\mathrm dt=0$ at each label.

Even when the source starts late, this velocity can already be present at time zero. For instance, in the endpoint problem over $U=0$ with a divergence-free source, $B_t=\bar f$ and $\int_0^T B\,\,\mathrm dt=0$, so $B(0)=-T^{-1}\int_0^T(T-t)\bar f(t)\,\,\mathrm dt$. We estimate the mean on $[0,T]$. The inverse preserves order in the scaled Hölder norms. Passing from supremum estimates for its source to those norms costs one derivative by (D.2).

