Start from $V_1,\mathsf p_1,C_2$ in (3.7) and put $B_1=0$, so that $Y_1=Z_1=V_1$. Set aside the activation error $R_{\widetilde\chi}$ of (3.8). The terms left by the leading wave at level two are $$\begin{equation}
 H_2=\widetilde\chi\bigl[(\partial_t+M)C[A_1]+\mathbf d\mathsf p[A_1]\bigr]
   +(V_1\cdot\mathbf d)V_1+(C_2\cdot\kappa)\partial_sV_1 . \tag{5.25}
\end{equation}$$ The linear term has zero phase mean. The apparent phase derivative in the last term also disappears on averaging. Indeed, (3.6) and integration over one period give $$\langle (C_2\cdot\kappa)\partial_sV_1\rangle_s
 =-\langle \partial_s(C_2\cdot\kappa)V_1\rangle_s
 =\langle (\mathbf d\cdot V_1)V_1\rangle_s.$$ Adding the other quadratic term gives the divergence of the averaged stress, as in the mean equation of Cheverry \[[34](#cite-Cheverry2006), Section 4.1, (4.19)\]. In components, $(\langle H_2\rangle_s)_\ell=\sum_i\mathbf d_i\langle (V_1)_\ell(V_1)_i\rangle_s$. There are two factors of $V_1$ and one derivative at fixed phase. By (5.22) and (D.3), $\|\langle H_2\rangle_s\|_{[n]}\le C_n\mathsf D^C\lambda^{-5/2}$. The residual $R_U\circ X$ has the same bound by (5.10). We include it in the first mean equation.

