**Proposition 8.1.** The data $U_j(0)$ converge in every $C^{m,1/2}$ to an odd divergence-free field $u_0$. In particular, $u_0\in C^\infty(\mathbb T^3;\mathbb R^3)$. Let $u$ be the maximal smooth solution of the Euler equations from $u_0$, with maximal time $T_*>0$, supplied by Section 1. Then $T_*\ge T_\infty$ and, for every $T<T_\infty$ and integer $m\ge0$, $U_j\to u$ in $C([0,T];C^{m,1/2})$. The solution is smooth and odd on $[0,T_\infty)\times\mathbb T^3$.

*Proof.* Fix $T<T_\infty$ and $m\ge1$. Since $t_j\uparrow T_\infty$ and $q_j\uparrow\infty$, we can choose $j_0$ with $T\le t_{j_0}$ and $q_{j_0}\ge m$. For $j>j_0$ the oscillation $O_j$ vanishes on $[0,T]$. Thus (7.4) gives, for $\ell>n\ge j_0$, $$\|U_\ell-U_n\|_{C([0,T];C^{m,1/2})}
 \le\sum_{j>n}\lambda_j^{-1}\le2\lambda_{n+1}^{-1}.$$ The limit in each of these norms is $w$. In particular $u_0=w(0)$ is smooth, and $w$ is odd and divergence free on $[0,T]$.

changes.adapted_text:
Growth at the launch times alone would still allow the curl to fall between launches. Fix a large $j$ and consider $[t_j,t_{j+1}]$. The preceding wave is fully active, and the new wave switches on during the first part of this interval. Applying <span class="upright">(T4)</span> of Theorem 7.1 at steps $j-1$ and $j$ gives, at the origin, $$\nabla U_j=\nabla U_{j-2}
   +\mathbf a_{j-1}\otimes\kappa_{j-1}
   +\widetilde\chi_j\,\mathbf a_j\otimes\kappa_j+E_{c,j-1}+E_{c,j}.$$ A gradient $\mathbf a\otimes\kappa$ contributes $\kappa\times\mathbf a$ to vorticity. At each time we project $\operatorname{curl}U_j$ along the actual wave vector $\kappa_j(t,0)$, which removes the leading curl of wave $j$. The preceding wave contributes $\kappa_{j-1}\times\mathbf a_{j-1}=\sigma_{j-1}E_3$ in its own frame. By (2.3) and Lemma 2.4, the $E_3$ component of $\kappa_j/\lambda_j$ is at least $1/2$ and its length is at most $C(1+\tau^2)$ at normalized time $\tau$. The normalized length of $[t_j,S_j]$ is at most $CL_{j+1}^4$ by item 2 of the proof of Theorem 7.1. Thus $$\begin{equation}
 \frac{\kappa_j}{|\kappa_j|}\cdot(\kappa_{j-1}\times\mathbf a_{j-1})
    \ge c\sigma_{j-1}L_{j+1}^{-8},\qquad
 \frac{\kappa_j}{|\kappa_j|}\cdot(\kappa_j\times\mathbf a_j)=0. \tag{8.1}
\end{equation}$$

