A mean $B=D_aX v$ with source $f_{\rm m}$ satisfies, by <span class="upright">(M.4.11)</span> with $g=(D_aX)^Tf_{\rm m}$ and $\mathcal T=\mathbb PK$ on divergence-free label fields, $\mathcal Tv_t=\mathbb P(g-\mathcal Dv)$. Hence $$\mathcal T\partial_t^{l+1}v=\mathbb P\Big[\partial_t^l g
 -\sum_{i=0}^l\binom li(\partial_t^i\mathcal D)\partial_t^{l-i}v
 -\sum_{i=1}^l\binom li(\partial_t^iK)\partial_t^{l+1-i}v\Big],$$ whose right side contains $v$ and the source only to time order $l$. By the last assertion of Theorem M.4.2, the constant $A$ of <span class="upright">(M.4.10)</span> bounds the inverse of $\mathcal T$ on the divergence-free fields of $C^{0,1/2}$ at each time. Then <span class="upright">(M.5.13)</span> is condition <span class="upright">(M.D.7)</span> for the equation $\mathcal Tw=\mathbb PF$ with the coefficient $K$, and Lemma M.D.3 in its Hölder form inverts $\mathcal T$ in the norms $\|\cdot\|_{[n],1/2}$ at every order, with a bound $C_q\mu^{a_q}$. Starting from the bound <span class="upright">(M.5.14)</span> for $v$, induction over $l\le q-1$ and <span class="upright">(M.D.2)</span> give, with the norms $\|\cdot\|_{[n]}$ on the left and $\|\cdot\|_{[n+1]}$ on the right, and independently of the depth, $$\begin{equation}
 \mathcal J_q(B)\le C_q\mu^{a_q}\mathcal J_{q-1}(f_{\rm m}). \tag{2.3}
\end{equation}$$ Thus the mean velocity at time order $q$ uses its source only to time order $q-1$, at the cost of one additional spatial derivative of the source. No boundary condition is imposed on time derivatives; the undifferentiated solution keeps its actual initial trace. A transverse solution satisfies $V_t=-(\operatorname{Id}-2P_\kappa)MV+(\operatorname{Id}-P_\kappa)f$; the same induction from <span class="upright">(M.5.21)</span> bounds its time derivatives in $\mathcal J_q$ by $C_q\mu^{a_q}$ times $\mathcal J_q(f)$, and <span class="upright">(M.5.16)</span> bounds its pressure. On $[t_0,T]$ the leading oscillation $A_1=g\mathbf aF_h$ solves this equation with $f=0$, and the cutoff of Subsection M.6.2 satisfies $|\widetilde\chi^{(l)}|\le C_lr^l$, with the rate $r$ of the comparison, and $r\le\mu$ by item 3 of the proof of Theorem M.7.1. With <span class="upright">(M.5.20)</span> and <span class="upright">(M.5.22)</span>, the same induction bounds $A_1$, $V_1=\widetilde\chi A_1$, their pressures and $R_{\widetilde\chi}=\widetilde\chi'(A_1+C[A_1])$ of <span class="upright">(M.3.8)</span> in the weighted $\mathcal J_q$ by $C_q\mu^{a_q}$ times $\lambda^{-5/4}$.

