At every fixed time order $q\ge1$ the induction over levels $p\ge2$ of <span class="upright">(M.5.40)</span> has the form $$\mathcal J_{q,n,\gamma}(f_p+f_p^S)+\lambda^{1/2}\mathcal J_{q,n}(B_p)
 \le C_{n,p,q}\lambda^{-5/4-2(p-1)/3}\qquad(n\ge0).$$ To see how the parent factors disappear, take $p=2$. The averaged stress and (2.2) bound $\mathcal J_{q-1,n+1}(\bar f_2)$ by $C_q\mu^{a_q}\lambda^{-5/2}$. The mean inverse (2.3) thus gives $\lambda^{1/2}\mathcal J_{q,n}(B_2)\le C_q\mu^{a_q}\lambda^{-2}$, where we have enlarged $a_q$. The three contributions to $f_2$ in <span class="upright">(M.5.25)</span> have respective powers $\lambda^{-9/4}$, $\lambda^{-5/2}$ and $\lambda^{-2}$. Their largest power is $\lambda^{-2}=\lambda^{-23/12}\lambda^{-1/12}$. The same spare $\lambda^{-1/12}$ occurs in the bound for the mean. Once it has absorbed the parent factor in that bound, the term $f_2^S=-(B_2\cdot\kappa)\partial_s V_1$ has a further margin $\lambda^{-1/4}$ relative to $\lambda^{-23/12}$.

The same margins recur at every level. The lower levels satisfy the displayed induction bound at every spatial order with no power of $\mu$. We estimate $\bar f_p$ at time order $q-1$ and one higher spatial order, then apply (2.3) before using $B_p$ in $f_p^S$. Each term uses a bounded number of the coefficient, product and inverse estimates, so their combined factor is $C_q\mu^{a_q}$ with $a_q$ independent of $p$ and $P$. The additional source $f_p^S$ has the margin $\lambda^{-1/4}$ from the proof of Theorem M.5.6. Even the smallest margin, $\lambda^{-1/12}$, absorbs the parent power for large $j$ since $\lambda=\mu^{K_j}$ and $K_j\to\infty$. Dependence on $n,p,q$ remains in $C_{n,p,q}$. This proves the induction bound and <span class="upright">(M.5.34)</span>–<span class="upright">(M.5.36)</span> in the time norms, with $C_q\mu^{a_q}$ in place of $\mathsf D^C$. The mean pressures do not enter this induction.

Summing to depth $P$ as in Theorem M.5.6 gives the respective bounds $C_{n,P,q}\mu^{a_q}\lambda^{-5/4}$ for the weighted norm of $W_{\rm osc}$, $C_{n,P,q}\mu^{a_q}\lambda^{-5/2}$ for the unweighted mean sum $\bar W$, and $C_{n,P,q}\lambda^{-5/4-2P/3}$ for the unweighted remainder $R_P$. The weighted norm of $R_{\widetilde\chi}$ is bounded by $C_q\mu^{a_q}\lambda^{-5/4}$. It and all its time derivatives vanish outside $[t_0,t_0+1/r]$.

*Eulerian time derivatives.* By $\partial_t=(\partial_t+U\cdot\nabla_x)-U\cdot\nabla_x$, the Eulerian derivative $\partial_t^if^\natural$ of a profile $f$, for $i\le q$, is a finite sum of products of factors $\partial_t^l\partial^\beta U$, with $l\le q-1$ and $|\beta|\le q-1$, and one factor $\partial_x^{\beta'}(\partial_t^lf)^\natural$ with $l+|\beta'|\le q$. Lemma M.5.7 converts the bounds in $\mathcal J_q$ to Eulerian $C^{n,1/2}$ bounds of the last factor, spending $n+|\beta'|+1$ label and phase derivatives. Each of these derivatives costs $C\mathsf D^C\lambda^{3/2}\le\lambda^2$ for an oscillatory profile, and $C\mathsf D^C$, a fixed power of $\mu$, for a phase-independent one. The extraction costs $q!T^{-q}$, a fixed power of $\mu$, and the factors of $U$ cost $\Lambda_U=\mu^5$ for each derivative, by (2.1).

For the phase-independent mean sum and fixed $(m,q)$ the conversion costs at most $C_q\mu^{a_q}$. The estimate therefore retains the factor $\lambda^{-5/2}$. For $j$ large, allowing the constant and exponent to depend also on $m$, we have $$\begin{equation}
 \|\partial_t^q\bar W^\natural\|_{C([0,S_j];C^{m,1/2})}
 \le C_q\mu^{a_q}\lambda^{-5/2}\le\tfrac12\lambda^{-1}, \tag{2.4}
\end{equation}$$ the last step because $\lambda=\mu^{K_j}$ and $K_j\to\infty$.

